---
## 4. Actions that require explicit human approval
Cursor must stop and request approval before:
- Committing code
- Pushing to GitHub
- Opening or updating a Pull Request
- Merging any branch
- Modifying main
- Rebasing shared branches
- Force pushing
- Resetting Git history
- Running git clean
- Deleting branches
- Deleting existing project files
- Replacing large sections of unrelated code
- Modifying .env.local
- Exposing or printing secrets
- Modifying Supabase tables
- Applying database migrations
- Changing RLS policies
- Deleting database records
- Connecting demo financial modules to live data
- Deploying to production
- Changing production configuration
- Installing a major framework or replacing an existing library
- Making any change outside the approved feature scope
Never treat silence as approval.
---
## 5. Git rules
- Never commit directly to main.
- Use one branch per feature.
- Recommended branch formats:
  feat/<feature-id>-<slug>
  fix/<issue-id>-<slug>
  chore/<task-id>-<slug>
  docs/<task-id>-<slug>
- Do not mix unrelated work in one commit.
- Do not discard uncommitted work.
- Do not overwrite another feature branch.
- Before creating a branch, inspect the current working tree.
- If unrelated work exists, preserve it and report the conflict before proceeding.
- Never merge without explicit human approval.
---
## 6. Scope control
Before implementation, identify:
- Approved objective
- Files likely to change
- Files that must not change
- Database impact
- External service impact
- Acceptance criteria
Do not add features that were not requested.
Do not redesign unrelated screens.
Do not perform broad cleanup unless it is necessary for the approved feature.
Do not remove existing CRM behaviour unless the approved specification explicitly requires it.
---
## 7. Data integrity rules
- Use real Supabase data only where existing tables support it.
- Never invent live business figures.
- Never present demo data as real business data.
- Label all placeholder data clearly as “Demo data”.
- Display “Not configured” where a real integration or data source does not exist.
- Do not invent revenue, commission, pipeline, customer or supplier figures.
- Do not modify Supabase during UI-only tasks.
- Database migrations must be separate, reviewable tasks.
- Never expose environment variables, API keys or service-role keys.
---
## 8. Financial module rules
For commissions, quotes, contracts, savings and forecasts:
- Keep calculations in named utility functions.
- Document calculation assumptions.
- Use decimal-safe financial handling where appropriate.
- Do not silently round intermediate values.
- Clearly distinguish expected, forecast, invoiced, paid and outstanding amounts.
- Demo figures must be visibly labelled.
- Live financial integrations require separate approval.
---
## 9. UI and code standards
Use:
- Next.js
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Existing AppShell
- Existing navigation and visual language
- Reusable components
- Accessible labels
- Semantic headings
- Responsive layouts
Avoid:
- One very large page component
- Duplicated business logic
- Inline mock data inside page components
- Unnecessary dependencies
- Unrelated visual redesigns
- Fake loading or fake live status indicators
Preferred structure:
- app route file
- components
- types
- constants
- utilities
- data loader
- tests where practical
---
## 10. Quality gates
Before reporting a feature complete, run:
```bash
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run build
```
---
## 11. Completion report
At the end of every implementation, report:
1. Current branch
2. Feature completed
3. Every file created
4. Every file modified
5. Every file deleted
6. Lint result
7. Typecheck result
8. Build result
9. Test result
10. Remaining warnings
11. Supabase impact
12. Environment impact
13. Unrelated routes touched
14. Exact local URL
15. Recommended manual tests
16. Whether the work is ready to commit
17. Any blockers or risks
Then stop and wait for approval.
---
## 12. Approval language
Treat these phrases as approval only for the named action:
- “Approved to implement”
- “Approved to commit”
- “Approved to push”
- “Approved to open the PR”
- “Approved to merge”
- “Approved to apply the migration”
- “Approved to deploy”
Approval for implementation does not include approval to commit.
Approval to commit does not include approval to push.
Approval to push does not include approval to merge.
Approval to merge does not include approval to deploy.
---
## 13. Efficient execution
Cursor should work continuously through all routine tasks inside the approved scope.
Do not stop after every file edit.
Do not ask repeated questions when the specification already answers them.
Batch related file edits.
Run quality checks after implementation rather than after every small edit.
Only interrupt for:
- A real scope conflict
- Possible loss of work
- Database changes
- Secret exposure
- Destructive commands
- Git history changes
- Commit, push, merge or deployment approval
- A business decision not covered by the specification
